== Version 0.13.0
[] Added Masada special building (3 tiers) at the capital of Negev: Ruins → Restored Fortifications → Grand Masada
[] Added nicknames for Kemet and Fertile Crescent founders: "the Lord of Kemet" and "Lord of the Crescent"
[] Improved nickname descriptions for the Rising Lion/Lioness and Beth Nahrain
[] Expanded Mishpacha government description with historical Nasi and Exilarchate context
[] Jewish-flavored Exilarchate election vote tooltips (Nasi, Melech, Nagid) replacing generic feudal terms
[] Added a new icon for Mishpacha Government, instead of administrative one.
[] Moving the domicile of Mishpacha government now uses the travel system.

== Version 0.14.0 ==
-- General --
[] Updated for Crusader Kings III 1.20 (faiths, rites and tenets moved to the new religion structure)
[] Jewish cultures now start with the same innovations as the people they lived among at every start date: Ashkenazi as the Rhineland Germans, Sephardi as the Castilians, Bavli, Radhanite and Hebrew as the Mashriqi, Kochinim as the Tamils, and Edomite as the Bedouin (Edomites were missing almost all innovations)
[] Copts now speak the vanilla Egyptian language instead of a separate Coptic one
[] Clearing the Temple grounds now removes Jerusalem as a holy site of the Islamic faiths
[] New stained-glass art for "The Jewish World" bookmark: a menorah between two lions of Judah on its tab, and a new start button
[] Compatible with Aligned Realm Banners: load Rising Lion after it to keep both mods' banner decorations
[] Rising Lion's events show its banner in the top right corner, as DLC events do

-- Mishpacha: government --
[] Rebalanced the government: mercenaries are 25% cheaper instead of more expensive, and families gain +15% income, +10% learning and stewardship lifestyle experience, +10 travel safety and +5 opinion from courtiers and guests
[] Removed the government's levy, knight and men-at-arms regiment and size penalties
[] Added Mishpacha Succession, fixing family heads and Exilarchs who had no working succession
[] Family heads are now titled Mar/Geveret whatever their liege (they were shown as the liege's Duke or High Chief); spouses are Geveret/Mar, and estates are "the X Household"
[] A family that gains land adopts its liege's government, or feudal if there is none
[] A family head who leaves Judaism steps down in favor of the nearest Jewish man of their house
[] Family heads have their own coat of arms shape, a banner hemmed like a Torah mantle, topped with a kippah instead of a rank crown; an Exilarch's is crowned with the Keter Shem Tov (the crown of a good name)

-- Mishpacha: realm and migration --
[] Families are now vassals of the realm their estate lies in, under a new "Charter of Privileges" contract (communal taxes, religious rights, Beit Din rights, trade rights); the title revocation protection option was removed
[] Families under a Muslim liege who taxes nonbelievers start paying jizya
[] A family whose estate lies under a duke or lesser ruler, at game start or after moving its estate, pays them tribute instead of staying independent. Independent and tributary families no longer lose their family title in the yearly cleanup
[] Moving the estate to another realm makes the family a vassal or tributary of that realm's ruler, ending its old tie
[] The estate can be moved from the province view, and while the realm is at war
[] Moving the estate takes longer (the whole family travels), and camp provision and herd travel events no longer fire for it
[] An estate that moves to a place with no city settles in the nearest city of that realm instead of being left behind
[] AI families move to more welcoming realms on their own, when the new home is clearly better than staying

-- Mishpacha: estate --
[] New estate building branches: Yeshiva (library), Beit Din (office) and Beit Knesset (replaces the Church and Monastery branches of the temple; protects against expulsion and roots the family)
[] The Counting House replaces the market, trading its influence for gold and training Court Financiers; the Guest Room gives piety instead of influence (welcoming guests is a mitzvah)
[] Mishpacha families have no influence, so the Temple, Crypt, Prison, Trophy Room and Silk Farm lines are replaced with copies that lack it, the Office line loses its governor bonuses, the Wine Cellar is hidden, and the Villa's influence becomes prestige (+1 to +3 per month by Villa tier); estate tooltips note which bonuses Mishpacha families don't get

-- Mishpacha: court --
[] Added Jewish court positions. Mishpacha families can appoint a Rosh Yeshiva, Av Beit Din, Paytan, Chazan, Gabbai Tzedakah, Shaliach, Sofer and Parnas, whose aptitude the matching estate buildings raise (shown in the estate view); landed Jewish rulers get the Av Beit Din and Shaliach, which no vanilla position covers for them
[] Jewish court positions have tasks. They share vanilla tasks with their counterparts (Teach Court, Write Poetry, Bolster Legitimacy, Divine Charity, the Chronicler's legend tasks, and for landed rulers Lead Public Sadaqah and Appease Zealots), and add Train Disciples, Judge the Community, Issue Responsa, Ransom Captives, Gather Donations, Bind the Communities, Keep the Estate's Affairs, Collect Dues and Petition the Authorities. Tasks from DLC content need that DLC. The Sofer is a Seneschal and Chronicler in one
[] The Jewish court positions and the Court Financier are full-time jobs: a character can hold only one of them, and not alongside a vanilla full-time position
[] At game start, families get the Jewish courtiers their court lacks (a scholar, a man of business, a go-between and a cantor), with the skills and traits the Jewish court positions look for

-- Mishpacha: contracts (Roads to Power) --
[] Player families get contract offers from nearby rulers every year, and when the estate moves or a new head takes over; offers not taken expire after about a year
[] Nine contracts, none martial: Extend a Loan, Farm the Tolls, Finance a Caravan, Act as Envoy, Redeem the Captives, Serve as Court Physician, Translate Texts, Answer a Responsum and Gather News from Correspondents
[] Each contract offers three or four approaches, each testing stewardship, diplomacy, learning or intrigue with its own risks and rewards (a bigger take or a scandal, a safe route or a fast one), and a results event tells you what came of it
[] Caravan, Envoy, Ransom, Translation and News run as contract schemes like an adventurer's, with agents, phases and the scheme windows; the approach sets the scheme's skill, agents and pace. The others are settled from the estate a few months on
[] The Counting House, Guest Room and Yeshiva and the Parnas, Shaliach, Rosh Yeshiva and Sofer improve the odds. One contract at a time, two with a Sofer; a Shaliach brings in an extra offer each year
[] AI families make money from a yearly business roll in place of contracts

-- Mishpacha: new families (Roads to Power) --
[] At game start, up to 60 new Jewish families settle in the cities of Christian, Muslim and Jewish realms across Europe, the Middle East and North Africa, each with a head aged 20 to 40, a spouse, children and an estate
[] Their culture follows the region (Sephardi in Iberia and the Maghreb, Ashkenazi in Latin Europe, Radhanite on the Khazar steppe, Bavli elsewhere); most are Rabbinic, with Karaites where their communities were strong

-- Jews in gentile realms --
[] Added the "Expel the Jews" decision for Christian and Muslim rulers. Jewish families leave the realm, Jewish courtiers are dismissed, Jewish lords lose their lands, and half of every departing Jew's gold is seized
[] Expelled Jews may resist in a single shared war; a lord who surrenders becomes a landless Mishpacha family. Expelled families can take refuge under dukes and lesser rulers too
[] Added the "Readmit the Jews" decision; edicts concerning the Jews have a 10-year cooldown
[] The AI decides on expulsion and readmission based on traits, rite tenets and personal tenets
[] Added notifications and a summary event for the ruler who expels the Jews
[] Added Shtadlanut: a Mishpacha family head can intercede with a ruler to readmit the Jews, grant a 15-year letter of protection against expulsion, free a Jewish prisoner, or (their own liege) waive jizya or lower communal taxes. A gift of gold, the family's prestige, a hook on the ruler, or owing them a favour (if they hold no hook on the family yet) make the ruler likelier to agree, against how hard the request is, their opinion of the family, how they feel about the Jews and how their faith views Judaism. Kings and emperors, rulers the family doesn't live under and rulers who granted a favour in the last 10 years are harder to win over; a Jewish Court Financier at their court and a petitioning Parnas help. Protected realms draw migrating families
[] Added the Court Financier court position for non-Jewish dukes and above: raises domain taxes and Jewish opinion, angers zealots, and brings debt-collection events

-- Kohenism --
[] Replaced Communal Identity with the new Kehunah tenet (hereditary priesthood, tithes), with its own icon. Only Jewish faiths and faiths with Jewish Syncretism can take it
[] The faith and its rite now agree on doctrines (the Kohen Gadol is a spiritual head)
[] New faith icon in the 1.20 style
[] High Priests keep the Kohen Gadol garments in their portraits after death, both historic ones and those who die in office during play

-- Fixes --
[] Fixed the Mishpacha family titles having no tier name, which flooded the error log
[] Fixed most faiths' rites being defined twice since CK3 1.20.1 renamed vanilla's file, which also brought back vanilla's rites for the Jewish faiths alongside the mod's
[] Fixed the Fertile Crescent region counting Levantine provinces twice
[] Fixed the Persian Struggle not including the Bavlim culture
[] Fixed many errors: Tanas holy site, Canaanite faith tenets, Lower Egypt adjective, Third Temple tenet check, Masada defense bonus, Maimonides' traits, Claim Exilarchate description, Semitic pagan religious head, and duplicate localization keys

== Version 0.15.0 ==
-- General --
[] The Semitic pagan religion is now called Levantine
[] Arameans, Assyrians and Nabati have place names of their own from Syria to Khuzestan, about 100 of them: Iron-Age Aramaic and Syriac for Arameans and Nabati (Arpad, Mabbug, Urhay, Ninwe, Arbel; Mesopotamia is Babylon), Neo-Assyrian for Assyrians (Bit Agusi, Ninua, Arbail, Karduniash)
[] Jewish and Canaanite place names corrected: Aleppo is Aram Zobah for Jews and Halab for Canaanites, the Jazira Aram Naharaim for Jews, for Canaanites Babylon is al-Hila, where its ruins lie, and Baghdad's duchy Akkad, and Assyrians no longer use Punic names in Tunisia
[] Aram, Osroene, Adiabene and the Palmyrene Empire carry arms of their own under Aramean and Assyrian holders, and Hadad's bull, the Abgarid tiara, Bel's eagle, Ishtar's star and the Mandaean drabsha appear in randomly generated arms
[] Added cultural men-at-arms: Palmyrene Archers (Arameans), Clibanarii (Assyrians), Bowmen of Seir (Edomites), the Sacred Band (Phoenicians and Carthaginians) and Nile Archers (Kemi and Ancient Egyptians)
[] The Mandaeans and the Nabati take part in the Persian struggle
[] Fixed Canaanism's gods showing as raw text keys, the Levantine religion missing the doctrines CK3 1.20 added, the Maimon family title's name, and log errors from the Shtadlanut files

-- Nabati --
[] Added the Nabati, the Aramaic-speaking peasants of the Sawad (Communal; Agrarian, Philosopher Culture, Language Scholars, Parochialism); at 867 they hold Wasit, Tib, the Marshes, Badaraya, Iskaf and Ubulla
[] The Shu'ubiyya: Nabati at Arab courts praise the Nabat over the Arabs, and Nabati under Arab rule take pride in Babylon
[] Copy the Nabataean Agriculture, a book of farming whose Babylonian lore may tempt its reader to the old gods

-- Mesopotamianism and Canaanism --
[] Added Mesopotamianism, the old gods of Assyria and Harran: a reformed pagan faith with two rites, Ashurism (Ashur, Marduk, Ishtar; the new Vicegerent of Ashur tenet) and Sinism (Sin of Harran; Astrology, Esotericism). Its holy sites are its temples: Harran, Ashur and Babylon (eminent), Nineveh, Arbela and Palmyra
[] Canaanism's main rite is now Baalism, the faith of the Phoenician cities; Punism is renamed Tanitism; and the new rite Yahwism is the old faith of Israel and Judah, Yahweh beside his Asherah, followed by the ancient kings who 'did evil in the eyes of the Lord'
[] New icons: the winged disc of Ashur, the crescent standard of Sin, Baal of the stele, the bull calf of Bethel and the sign of Tanit, with colours for custom faiths
[] New Mesopotamian and Canaanite rites are named after a god, by their founder's city or their tenets (Mardukism, Belism, Hadadism, Chemoshism, Melqartism, Astartism and 24 more), each with the god's own icon
[] Court chaplains have their own titles: Kumra, Shangu and Entu for Mesopotamianism, Kohen, Rab Kohanim, Komer and Kohenet for Canaanism
[] Kohenism, Canaanism and Mesopotamianism are closed to converts until restored: the Third Temple, Phoenicia or Carthage, and the Temple of Sin

-- Temples of the old gods --
[] New great projects: the Temple of Sin at Harran, which restores the old gods, then for independent rulers of the old faith the Temples of Bel at Palmyra, Shamash at Hatra, Ishtar at Arbela and Ashur at Ashur, and the Esagila, Marduk's temple at Babylon
[] Dig into the mound of Nineveh again and again for the tablets of Ashurbanipal's library

-- Empires --
[] Added the Palmyrene Empire: an independent Aramean king of Palmyra and Syria raises the empire of Odaenathus and Zenobia, taking Syria and whatever Jazira, Jerusalem, Israel, Phoenicia, Arabia and Egypt they completely control
[] Beth Nahrain's capital is now Nineveh, and the Fertile Crescent absorbs the Palmyrene Empire

-- Festivals --
[] Festivals are new kinds of feast, each shown only to its faith and in its season: Akitu for Mesopotamian pagans, the Feast of Nisan for Sabians and Sinites, Parwanaya and Dehwa Rabba for Mandaeans (on the Mandaean calendar, which drifts a day every four years) and Pesach for every Jewish faith
[] Pesach has its own seder for Karaites, Samaritans and the Beta Israel, more for an Exilarch, a ruler of Jerusalem and a rebuilt Temple, and every Jewish ruler without a grand seder keeps a household one
[] Mesopotamian rulers who let Akitu lapse for 8 years lose legitimacy and piety

-- Mandaeans --
[] The Rish Amma heads the Mandaeans at every start date, with a coat of arms; when one dies a Ganzibra takes the title if there is one
[] New holy sites: Tib and the Marshes (eminent), Harran, Basra and Shushtar; and a new icon, the drabsha
[] 867: Tib and the Marshes are Mandaean, and Harran Sabian
[] Receive the Masbuta, baptism in living water, if your seat or estate lies on a river; copy the Ginza Rabba, signed by its copyist; and hear the Haran Gawaita, the legend of the people's wanderings

-- Sabians --
[] 867: Thabit ibn Qurra, the mathematician and translator, heads a playable Sabian family in Baghdad; Jabir ibn Sinan and his son al-Battani live at Harran
[] Added the Court Astrologer, a Sabian of Harran who reads the stars for Muslim, Sabian and Middle Eastern courts: horoscopes for wars, births and marriages, astronomical tasks, and an option in Recruit for a Court Position to send to Harran for one
[] The Question of the Sabians: a Muslim ruler can make the Sabians and Mandaeans of the realm prove themselves a people of the Book, as al-Ma'mun did at Harran; they pay, defend the claim or convert
[] Muslim rulers may be offered a Sabian scholar's learning: translations of the Greeks, or the realm's sums
[] Added the Harran dynasty legacy for Sabian and old-faith dynasties

-- The Return of the Moon God --
[] A story for Thabit ibn Qurra's house in five acts, shown in the story window, its decisions notifying you when they can be taken:
[] Act I, The Sabians of Baghdad: raise the Sabians' standing with translations, horoscopes and cures for Muslim patrons, and keep the old gods in secret
[] Act II, The Return to Harran: petition for Harran, buy its tax farm, or raise the Harranians in war
[] Act III, Throw Off the Caliph: ask your liege to let Harran go, or fight for it
[] Act IV, Restore the Old Gods: rebuild the Temple of Sin and unveil the mysteries; every Sabian turns to Sinism, the Mandaeans disown Harran and the Muslims resent it
[] Act V, Reclaim the Land: claim the old gods' cities and bring their statues home, until all six gods are home or the Palmyrene Empire or Beth Nahrain is raised

-- Familial (was Mishpacha) --
[] Mishpacha is now Familial, a government for landless families of any faith; Jewish families keep their Mar/Geveret titles, kippah, court positions, Yeshiva, Beit Din, Beit Knesset, contracts and Shtadlanut, and others are headed by a Mar or Marta
[] Every start has a few Sabian families (around Harran, before 1100) and Mandaean ones (the marshes and Khuzestan)
[] Sabian and Mandaean families have court positions of their own (Mu'allim, Ra'is al-Kahana, Kahin and others; Ganzibra, Tarmida and others), estate branches (Diwan, Haykal; Scriptorium, Mandi), contracts told in their own words, and the crescent of Sin or the klila on their arms
[] Sabian families can employ a Court Astrologer, whom their Observatory trains, and take a new contract, Cast a Horoscope
[] Sabian and Mandaean families can plead to be recognised as a people of the Book, sparing them the Question for 20 years
[] A head who leaves the family's religion steps down for the nearest man of the house who keeps it
[] Family heads can hold feasts at their estate
[] Taking a loan, tolls, physician, responsum or horoscope contract tells you what comes next
[] Fixed old estate temples not turning into the family's own temple branch
[] Saves from earlier versions are not compatible
